 ## Overview
-Sparse Voxel Traversal is a hierarchical ray-intersection method that accelerates volume rendering by recursively traversing a sparse voxel octree (SVO), skipping empty regions to reduce sampling cost [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of dense voxel traversal by allowing the ray to take larger steps through empty space, significantly reducing the total number of intersection tests and node visits required to reach active data [S1]. Unlike flat grid traversal which checks every voxel, hierarchical traversal skips entire subtrees that do not intersect the ray or are marked inactive, enabling efficient rendering of sparse or multi-resolution datasets [S1]. It differs from its siblings by explicitly tracking spatial bounds and using recursive descent with mid-plane comparisons to select child nodes, rather than relying on fixed-step iteration or hash-based lookups [S2, unofficial].
+Sparse Voxel Traversal is a hierarchical ray-intersection method that accelerates volume rendering by recursively traversing a sparse voxel octree (SVO) to find intersections with leaf nodes or branches, skipping empty regions to reduce sampling cost [S1][S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of dense voxel traversal by allowing the ray to take larger steps through empty space, significantly reducing the total number of intersection tests and node visits required to reach active data [S1]. Unlike [Voxel Grid Raycasting](2218-voxel-grid-raycasting.md) which steps through every voxel boundary in a uniform grid, this method uses hierarchical spatial subdivision to find non-empty space faster [S1]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit use of regular 2x2x2 subdivision and mid-plane sorting logic for interior nodes, whereas other methods may focus purely on traversal optimization or specific data structures like hash grids [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
